        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The National Academy of Sciences rated the loss of bone, due to conditions of microgravity, as one of the most important health-related factors limiting the future of manned space exploration. The mechanisms of bone loss, however, are poorly understood. The investigators hypothesized that a primary increase in bone resorption (the release of calcium from the bones) would occur due to the microgravity environment, and that there would be a normal adaptive response from the hormones that control resorption.
<p>
The purpose of this experiment was to develop a model of calcium and bone metabolism during space flight, to help explain the disorders of mineral metabolism during weightlessness: hypercalciuria (the presence of abnormally high calcium levels in the urine), mild hypercalcemia (the presence of abnormally high calcium levels in the blood), and osteopenia (decreased bone density).  The investigators wanted to determine whether changes in calcium turnover and/or changes in calciotropic hormones occurred.  The body requires a constant supply of calcium to control and maintain extracellular concentrations of this mineral.  Calcium concentration is controlled mainly by a calciotropic hormone called parathyroid hormone (PTH); this hormone affects bone reabsorption, a process in which calcium is freed from bone and enters into free circulation in the body.  Normally, serum ionized calcium and intact PTH are negatively correlated:  when the level of calcium in the blood is low, the concentration of PTH will be high in response.  Any changes in the PTH/calcium balance during space flight were expected to be in the normal physiological range of responses.
<p>
The investigators sought to determine whether calcium turnover and bone resorption change during space flight; and if so, when such changes begin and whether they are primary or secondary to changes in bone metabolism.  (Calcium absorption could not be studied on SLS-1).  The investigators also sought to determine whether blood levels of PTH and other hormones that influence calcium levels change significantly during space flight; and if so, whether the changes occur in response to a change in the normal balance of minerals (including calcium) in the bones.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
On both flights, urine samples, fecal samples, and blood samples were collected.  Serum calcium and calcium regulatory hormones were measured in preflight, inflight and postflight blood samples.  Levels of pyridinoline crosslinks (indicators of bone resorption) were measured in urine collected throughout the study, and a record of the subjects’ food and fluid intake and use of medication was kept throughout the study. The investigators performed a statistical analysis of the relationship between serum ionized calcium and calcium-regulating hormones before, during and after the mission.
<p>
An intestinal absorption study was performed only on SLS-2; the double-isotope tracer method was used to determine intestinal calcium absorption.  In this method, two stable isotopes of calcium (calcium-46 and calcium-48) were used.  This method involved oral ingestion of calcium-48, followed some time later by an intravenous injection of calcium-46.  The ratio of the oral to intravenous tracers in the blood indicates the fraction of calcium load absorbed.  The study was performed 19 days before flight, and during flight on days 3 and 12.
<p>
RESULTS: <br>
On SLS-1, serum ionized calcium increased dramatically on flight day 2 (FD2) to levels of 30-40% above control, levels that are normally considered to be representative of severe hypercalcemia. The increase persisted until FD8, and made a rapid return to baseline values postflight (partially on the day after landing, and completely by 6 days after landing). Before the flight of SLS-1, it was expected that any changes due to space flight would be in the normal physiological range of responses.  Therefore, the magnitude of the increased serum calcium was surprising, since these levels are normally associated with hypercalcemia, and since these levels would usually cause symptoms of nausea and malaise, which were not seen on SLS-1.  The same trend was observed on SLS-2, but was smaller in magnitude, attributed to the fact that the baseline PTH values for the SLS-2 subjects were lower to begin with.  On SLS-2, an unexplained decrease in serum pH was seen and was thought to be a possible effect on the measurement of serum ionized calcium.  Upon further analysis, the decreased pH was attributed to an increase in the carbon dioxide content of the blood.  This effect was only seen in flight samples and the investigators have been unable to reproduce the effect in the laboratory.  
<p>
Serum PTH showed a normal adaptive response to the increased calcium levels.  Mean serum intact PTH declined rapidly by 20-30% on FD2 and persisted through FD8; it slowly returned to baseline values by 6 days after landing.  This is a finding that biologically validates the increase in serum ionized calcium, and discounts the possibility that PTH is responsible for the hypercalcemia.  Another unexpected finding was the much tighter correlation between PTH and serum ionized calcium seen in the male subjects than in the female subjects, a difference which had not been previously noted in the literature (but which had also not been studied).
<p>
On SLS-1 flight day 2, blood albumin levels were also low, possibly explaining part of the increase in ionized calcium in the presence of relatively normal total serum calcium.
<p>
Serum 1,25 dihydroxyvitamin D (1,25(OH)2D) was within the range of control on FD 2 but had declined by 40% by FD8 (SLS-1) and by 30-40% by FD6 (SLS-2). This was an expected response to the decrease in PTH.  
<p>
SLS-2 results show that decreased intestinal calcium absorption does occur in flight.
<p>
Serum osteocalcin, only measured on SLS-2, declined rapidly in flight and remained depressed.  Since osteocalcin is an indicator of bone formation, this could signify decreased bone formation in flight.  However, osteocalcin is also depressed by cortisol, so a stress response to space flight cannot be ruled out as the cause.
<p>
Urine deoxypyridinoline, only measured on SLS-2, increased approximately 40% in flight.  The level climbed rapidly between FD4 and 8, and remained at that level until 2 weeks after landing.  This is the first evidence that there is a dramatic increase in bone resorption in humans during space flight which causes a rise in serum ionized calcium.  It is consistent with the timeframe needed to recruit new osteoclasts from the bone marrow and to increase the number of bone resorbing sites.
<p>
In conclusion, the endocrine mechanisms of calcium metabolism appear to occur normally in space flight.  The magnitude and timing of the increase in serum ionized calcium indicate that processes other than bone resorption also influence the levels of serum calcium.  This is clarified by the fact that the rise in serum ionized calcium occurs early, and several days before the evidence for bone resporption appears.  This could be due to a direct release of calcium from the bone fluid compartment.  It could also be influenced by a decrease in the rate of mineralization of bone while resorption and intestinal absorption are still at normal levels.
<END>
